**`crypto.randomUUID()`** returns a randomly generated v4 UUID as a 36-character string, using a cryptographically secure random number generator.

```js
let uuid = self.crypto.randomUUID();
console.log(uuid); // e.g. "36b8f84d-df4e-4d49-b662-bcde71a8764f"
```

It takes no parameters.

**Secure context requirement:** The method is available only in secure contexts (HTTPS) in some or all supporting browsers. On plain HTTP pages it may be undefined, so check for it before calling it:

```js
if (globalThis.crypto?.randomUUID) {
  const id = crypto.randomUUID();
} else {
  // Fallback, e.g. build a UUID from crypto.getRandomValues()
}
```

**Web Workers:** The feature is also available in Web Workers, through `self.crypto`.

**Browser support:** MDN lists the feature as Baseline "Widely available" and says it has been available across browsers since March 2022. The page content you provided doesn't include the compatibility table, so I can't confirm the exact Safari iOS version from it. From my general knowledge, Safari added support in 15.4, which shipped in March 2022, so iOS 15.4 and later should support it. Check the [MDN browser compatibility table](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Crypto/randomUUID#browser_compatibility) to confirm.

